WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.738
CASE creates derived values without modifying the
stored table PostgreSQL checks WHEN

00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:09.625
clauses from top to bottom As soon as one WHEN
condition is

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:14.738
true CASE stops and returns that branch's THEN
expression Later branches are

00:00:14.762 --> 00:00:19.225
not evaluated for that row If no WHEN matches ELSE
is used

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.700
if ELSE is omitted the result is NULL This makes
branch order

00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:27.425
part of the rule A broad branch placed too early can
make

00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:33.050
a more specific branch unreachable Before writing
CASE identify overlapping conditions

00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:38.512
and decide which outcomes have higher priority
Specific exceptions and higher thresholds

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.625
usually belong before broader defaults
